    public DocumentDefinition Create() => DocumentDefinition.Create(ScribeNode.Create(
        "The original integer chart carries the finite actual modular-zero partner set. In the regular dividing branch, the three modular determinant equations lift to exact integer equations under 8J₁J₂<p, giving a signed divisor bound. In the nondividing branch, formal polynomial cancellation classifies every field-coordinate partner among six ordered pairs; coordinate reduction is injective on the bounded integer box, so at most six actual pairs remain.",
        H("Modular-zero partner count: determinant component"),
        Blocks(
            Paragraph(Text("The source is the supplied proof.tex, Lemma modular-zero partner bound, lines 235–370, SHA256 81da80fcfc8b93fadb2b5bb12d99f7ea4ea7a8b4c1cd95594e763e378bc21f7f. The definitions retain Original.Coordinate, Original.Endpoint and the actual original endpointL, endpointQ and pencil. ActualPair uses u(i,j)=α+i+βj and the equation u(x)u(y)=α(α+q₁+βq₂).")),
            Paragraph(Text("For integer directions f,h with det(f,h)≠0 and AC=det(q,h)det(q,f)≠0, an oriented pair (z,v) satisfies three modular equations: det(f,z)=0, det(h,v−q)=0 and det(z−q,v)=0. The direction bounds are |f₁|≤J₁, |f₂|≤J₂, |h₁|≤2J₁, |h₂|≤2J₂; q,z,v lie in the original rectangle. Their determinant heights are at most 2J₁J₂, 8J₁J₂ and 4J₁J₂. Thus 8J₁J₂<p forces all three integer determinants to vanish.")),
            Paragraph(Text("The map (z,v)↦(det(z−q,h),det(f,v)) lands in Int.divisorsAntidiag(AC). Its injectivity follows from det(f,h)≠0 and the two line equations, without choosing primitive integer generators. Consequently the oriented count is at most the cardinality of that signed divisor antidiagonal, and allowing both endpoint orientations gives twice that cardinality. The constant satisfies |AC|≤8(J₁J₂)²<p².")),
            Paragraph(Text("The regular dividing branch supplies both modular line orientations, a divisor count for the actual pencil, and a uniform prime subpower bound under explicit direction hypotheses. The nondividing branch has an absolute six-partner bound under nonzero det(a,a+b−q) and det(a+b−q,b), using injectivity of full coordinate reduction on the bounded box. The original-source nondegeneracy supplier and combination of branches belong to PascadiOriginalModularPartners. These counts are intermediate components of the B⁵ prime-count prerequisite.")),
            Paragraph(Text("In the regular dividing branch, q=b+(1−c)a and det(b,q)≠0. Perpendicular evaluation of the homogeneous pencil identity assigns z or v to the b-line. Writing that endpoint as k b and comparing all three quadratic coefficients gives (ck−1)v=kb−q. Since det(b,q)≠0, ck−1 is nonzero. The relation then yields det(a−q,v−q)=0 and det(z−q,v)=0, or their swapped counterparts. The original mapped pencil supplies the homogeneous identity after cancelling its nonzero base linear factor, including constant and vertical directions. Consequently actual modular-zero partners in this branch satisfy the two-orientation divisor count.")),
            Paragraph(Text("For the nondividing identity L_d L_z L_v=(L_z+L_v−L_q)L_a L_b with d=a+b−q, assume det(a,d) and det(d,b) are nonzero. Formal cancellation handles zero endpoints, yielding only (0,q) and (q,0). For nonzero endpoints, evaluating at a perpendicular direction puts one endpoint on the a-line; after cancelling L_a, evaluation at a perpendicular direction to b puts the other on the b-line. Write z=ka and v=lb. Coefficient comparison gives (kl−1)d=(k−1)a+(l−1)b. Its determinants force either k=l=1 or k=(det(a,b)−det(d,b))/det(a,d) and l=(det(a,b)−det(a,d))/det(d,b). Thus six ordered field pairs suffice, including proportional a,b and characteristic two. For bounded integer endpoints, J₁,J₂≥1 and 8J₁J₂<p make coordinate reduction injective, giving the actual count at most six without injectivity of the single shifted value α+i+βj.")),
            Describe.Lean(DescribeId.Create("pascadi-modular-zero-coordinate-det"),
